    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Type 2 diabetes is diagnosed using specific blood tests performed in a clinical or laboratory setting. In most cases without severe symptoms, the test must be repeated on a second day to confirm the diagnosis.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "using specific blood tests performed in a clinical or laboratory setting"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Diagnostic Blood Tests"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Hemoglobin A1C Test: Measures your average blood sugar level over the past 2 to 3 months.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 6.5% or higher"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 5.7% to 6.4%",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  1,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG) Test: Measures your blood sugar after fasting (no food or drink except water) for at least 8 hours.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) or higher"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 100 to 125 mg/dL (5.6 to 6.9 mmol/L)",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  1,
                  2,
                  3,
                  4
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT): Measures your blood sugar before and 2 hours after you drink a special sweet glucose liquid (after an 8-hour fast).",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L) or higher at the 2-hour mark"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 140 to 199 mg/dL (7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L) at the 2-hour mark",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  1,
                  4
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Random Plasma Glucose Test: Measures your blood sugar at any time of day, regardless of when you last ate.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 200 mg/dL or higher, typically used when classic symptoms of high blood sugar (such as extreme thirst, frequent urination, or unexplained weight loss) are present.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  5,
                  6,
                  7
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Confirmation and Screening Guidelines"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Repeat Testing: If you do not have clear, classic symptoms of high blood sugar, doctors generally require a repeat test (using the same or a different test) on a separate day to confirm a type 2 diabetes diagnosis. If you have severe symptoms and a very high blood sugar reading, a single test may be enough .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Routine Screening: The American Diabetes Association recommends routine screening for all adults starting at age 35, or earlier if you have risk factors such as overweight/obesity, a family history of diabetes, high blood pressure, or a history of gestational diabetes.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "American Diabetes Association",
                "link": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/diagnosis"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "More details on testing and criteria can be reviewed through the MedlinePlus Diabetes Type 2 Guide .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "MedlinePlus Diabetes Type 2 Guide",
            "link": "https://medlineplus.gov/diabetestype2.html"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          6
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you or a loved one experiencing any symptoms (like frequent thirst or fatigue)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have risk factors like family history or prediabetes?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can share what to expect during your doctor's visit or how to prepare for a fasting blood test."
      }
    ],
